title: Host linear algebra
summary: skoro-dense. f64 dense factorizations, and GTSAM-style block-sparse elimination of a linear-Gaussian factor graph (COLAMD ordering, junction tree, multifrontal QR, Bayes tree, marginals) on the host via faer; and skoro-linearize, the bridge from a model.
order: 10
---

`skoro-dense` is host-resident linear algebra in `f64`, backed by [faer](https://faer.veganb.tw/).
It depends only on `skoro-backend` and `skoro-core`, so nothing that merely uses `Normal` pays for
`faer`.

```
{../../../../crates/skoro-dense/src/lib.rs:1..4}
```

## Dense

```
{../../../../crates/skoro-dense/src/lib.rs:8..14}
```

`skoro_dense::factor` has the dense entry points, all in the `skoro` prelude and all returning
`SkoroResult`: `cholesky`, `solve_lower`, `solve_upper`, `spd_solve`, `lstsq`, and
`whitening_from_precision`. The last turns a precision $\Lambda$ a caller already has into a
lower-triangular $W$ with $W\transpose W = \Lambda$, packed for a `Tril` quantity — the whitening of a
hand-built joint factor. It takes one Cholesky of $\Lambda$ with its rows and columns reversed, and
inverts nothing.

## Sparse, by blocks

A model's linearization is block-sparse: each term touches a few quantities. `skoro-dense`
eliminates it the way GTSAM does, with keys, not scalars, as the unit:

```
{../../../../crates/skoro-dense/src/lib.rs:18..33}
```

- **Ordering.** Fill-in, and so cost, is decided by the elimination order. COLAMD runs on the
  *block* pattern, one entry per factor and key: every scalar column of a key shares its sparsity,
  so ordering keys is cheaper and loses nothing. `Ordering::ConstrainedLast(keys)` puts chosen keys
  last, which is how a marginal over them is computed.
- **The symbolic IR.** A `JunctionTree` records, for each clique of keys eliminated together, its
  separator — the keys its elimination leaves a factor on — without a single number. It is computed
  once per structure and reused across solver iterations, the rows of a batch, and the ticks of a
  filter whose topology did not change; it checks the graph's `Structure` on use rather than
  trusting it.
- **Numeric elimination.** Each clique stacks its factors and its children's separator factors
  into a dense $[A_f \mid A_s \mid b]$ and factors it by *unpivoted* Householder QR. The top rows
  are a conditional $R x_f + S x_s = d$; the next rows are the new separator factor, handed up. A
  front that cannot determine a key is `RankDeficient` (gauge freedom, an unobserved direction).
- **Partial elimination is lazy.** Eliminating only some keys touches only the factors adjacent to
  them. What is left on the kept keys is the Schur complement
  $\Lambda_{kk} - \Lambda_{ke}\Lambda_{ee}^{-1}\Lambda_{ek}$ in square-root form, and one more QR —
  on the columns reversed, then flipped back — makes it the lower-triangular $W$ a `Tril` holds.

From a complete elimination, a `BayesTree` gives the least-squares step (`back_substitute`), the
Fisher step against a gradient (`solve_normal`, $(R\transpose R)^{-1} g$), draws from the Laplace
approximation (`solve_upper`, $R^{-1} z$), and $\tfrac12\ln\det A\transpose A$ (`log_det_r`).

## What the rows mean is the caller's business

Nothing above asks where a factor's rows came from. A whitened residual Jacobian with $b = -r$, a
Fisher root $C_t J_t$ with $b = 0$, and a damping prior $\sqrt\lambda I$ are the same object, a
factor contributing $A\transpose A$ to a curvature. `skoro-linearize` builds the graph from a model:

- `linearize(model, points, free, Curvature::Residual)` reads each term's `RESIDUAL`: the
  Gauss–Newton rows. It refuses a term whose normalizer depends on a free quantity — a Gaussian
  whose covariance is being estimated — since $\tfrac12\lVert r\rVert^2$ is then not its negative
  log-density.
- `linearize(model, points, free, Curvature::Fisher)` reads each term's `FISHER_ROOT`, with the
  gradient of $\log p$ alongside: correct for any distribution that binds a root, heteroscedastic
  Gaussians included.
- `marginalize_out(model, points, free, &[x], curvature)` is what retiring `x` leaves on its Markov
  blanket, from `x`'s own factors alone, and `joint_normal_from` turns it into the `JointNormal`
  that replaces them — with the block order taken from the marginal, never supplied separately.

Each term's rows cover only the free quantities it reaches. `jacobian_blocks` gets them in one
reverse pass by colouring rows whose supports are disjoint into shared frames, so a chain of any
length needs a handful of frames rather than one per row.

## Why QR on the host

The damped step solves

$$
(J\transpose J + \lambda I)\,\delta = -J\transpose r
\iff
\min_\delta \left\lVert \begin{bmatrix} J \\ \sqrt\lambda\, I \end{bmatrix}\delta - \begin{bmatrix} -r \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} \right\rVert .
$$

Solving the right-hand form by QR keeps the conditioning at $\kappa$ instead of $\kappa^2$ at *every*
damping value, and burn has no QR. That settles where the solve runs. The Jacobian blocks cross in,
the step crosses out, and everything between is host `f64` with no representation in the graph.

> [!DECISION]
> See [Solves on the host](../../design/host-dense-linear-algebra/).

## Keeping two triangular solves apart

`TrilInvert` (in `skoro-linalg`) is a differentiable graph operation on every backend, and it stays
there. `skoro-dense`'s triangular solve is for estimators. A shared test checks that the two compute
the same inverse.
